  </div>

  <!-- AEO Top Answer Summary Box -->
  <section class="acdt-intro content-section" aria-label="Quick Answer Summary">
    <div class="info-callout speakable-summary">
      <h3>Quick Summary: How the International Date Line Works</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>The International Date Line (IDL) is an internationally recognized boundary located roughly at 180° longitude in the Pacific Ocean that separates two consecutive calendar dates.</strong> 
        When traveling <strong>WESTWARD</strong> across the line (from the US toward Asia/Australia), you <strong>ADD one calendar day (+1 day)</strong>, stepping into tomorrow. 
        When traveling <strong>EASTWARD</strong> across the line (from Asia/Australia toward the US), you <strong>SUBTRACT one calendar day (-1 day)</strong>, stepping back into yesterday. 
        The line is not straight; it zigzags around island nations like Kiribati and Samoa so that whole territories share the same calendar day as their primary economic partners.
      </p>
    </div>
    
    <div class="reading-meta" style="margin-top: 1rem; color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.9rem;">
      <span>⏱️ Estimated Reading Time: <strong>16 minutes</strong> | 🌏 Geography: <strong>Mid-Pacific 180° Meridian Navigation</strong></span>
    </div>
  </section>

