  <!-- SECTION 1 -->
  <section id="definition-geography" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Definition and Geographic Location">
    <h2>1. Definition &amp; Geographic Location (180° Meridian)</h2>
    <p>
      The Earth rotates 360 degrees in 24 hours. If you travel around the world continuously eastward, setting your clock forward 1 hour for every 15 degrees of longitude, by the time you return to your starting point, your local clock will have accumulated 24 extra hours—putting you 1 full day ahead of the people who stayed behind!
    </p>
    <p>
      To prevent calendar chaos, world nations established an international consensus line situated exactly opposite the <a href="/what-is-utc-offset/">Prime Meridian (0° longitude)</a> in Greenwich, London: the <strong>180th Meridian</strong> in the Pacific Ocean.
    </p>
    <p>
      This line serves as the global reset button for calendar dates. Read more in <a href="/why-time-zones-exist/">Why Time Zones Exist</a> and <a href="/how-time-zones-work/">How Time Zones Work</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- SVG IDL Map Diagram -->
  <section id="svg-idl-map" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Visual Diagram International Date Line Map">
    <h2>2. Visualizing the International Date Line Shift</h2>
    <p>
      The diagram below illustrates how calendar dates switch when crossing the 180-degree meridian:
    </p>

    <div class="infographic-wrapper">
      <svg class="visual-svg" viewBox="0 0 800 340" width="100%" height="auto" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-label="International Date Line Directional Date Shift Map">
        <rect width="800" height="340" rx="12" fill="#0f172a" stroke="var(--line)" stroke-width="2"/>
        
        <!-- Center Zigzag Date Line -->
        <path d="M 400 30 L 400 100 L 470 160 L 400 230 L 400 310" stroke="#f59e0b" stroke-width="4" stroke-dasharray="6" fill="none"/>
        <text x="400" y="25" fill="#fbbf24" font-family="Space Grotesk, sans-serif" font-weight="700" font-size="14" text-anchor="middle">INTERNATIONAL DATE LINE (180°)</text>

        <!-- West Side (Asia / Australia) -->
        <rect x="50" y="60" width="310" height="230" rx="8" fill="rgba(16, 185, 129, 0.1)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="2"/>
        <text x="205" y="95" fill="#34d399" font-family="Space Grotesk, sans-serif" font-weight="700" font-size="18" text-anchor="middle">WEST SIDE (Asia / NZ)</text>
        <text x="205" y="125" fill="#e2e8f0" font-size="14" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">CALENDAR DATE: TOMORROW</text>
        <text x="205" y="155" fill="#94a3b8" font-size="13" text-anchor="middle">Example: Wednesday, July 30</text>
        <text x="205" y="185" fill="#34d399" font-size="12" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">Earliest Zones: UTC+12 to UTC+14</text>
        <text x="205" y="215" fill="#cbd5e1" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle">Tokyo, Sydney, Auckland, Kiribati</text>

        <!-- East Side (Americas / Hawaii) -->
        <rect x="440" y="60" width="310" height="230" rx="8" fill="rgba(59, 130, 246, 0.1)" stroke="#3b82f6" stroke-width="2"/>
        <text x="595" y="95" fill="#60a5fa" font-family="Space Grotesk, sans-serif" font-weight="700" font-size="18" text-anchor="middle">EAST SIDE (Americas / Hawaii)</text>
        <text x="595" y="125" fill="#e2e8f0" font-size="14" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">CALENDAR DATE: YESTERDAY</text>
        <text x="595" y="155" fill="#94a3b8" font-size="13" text-anchor="middle">Example: Tuesday, July 29</text>
        <text x="595" y="185" fill="#60a5fa" font-size="12" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">Latest Zones: UTC-10 to UTC-12</text>
        <text x="595" y="215" fill="#cbd5e1" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle">Honolulu, <a href="/los-angeles-time/">Los Angeles</a>, Baker Island</text>

        <!-- Crossing Arrows -->
        <path d="M 340 130 L 460 130" stroke="#60a5fa" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#arrow-idl)"/>
        <text x="400" y="120" fill="#60a5fa" font-size="10" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">EASTBOUND: SUBTRACT 1 DAY (-1d)</text>

        <path d="M 460 190 L 340 190" stroke="#34d399" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#arrow-idl)"/>
        <text x="400" y="180" fill="#34d399" font-size="10" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">WESTBOUND: ADD 1 DAY (+1d)</text>

        <defs>
          <marker id="arrow-idl" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="5" refY="5" markerWidth="5" markerHeight="5" orient="auto-start-reverse">
            <path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" fill="#fbbf24" />
          </marker>
        </defs>
      </svg>
      <p class="infographic-caption">Figure 1: International Date Line Crossing Rules (Adding +1 Day Westbound / Subtracting -1 Day Eastbound).</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 3 -->
  <section id="directional-rules" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Directional Date Shift Rules">
    <h2>3. Directional Date Shift Rules (Westbound vs Eastbound)</h2>
    <p>
      Remembering which way to adjust your calendar when crossing the IDL is simple:
    </p>

    <div class="info-callout">
      <h3>The Golden Rule of Date Line Crossing</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Travelling WEST (US $\to$ Asia):</strong> You cross into the future! <strong>Add 1 calendar day (+1 Day).</strong> (Tuesday becomes Wednesday).</li>
        <li><strong>Travelling EAST (Asia $\to$ US):</strong> You cross into the past! <strong>Subtract 1 calendar day (-1 Day).</strong> (Wednesday becomes Tuesday).</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 4 -->
  <section id="history-magellan" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="History and Magellan 1522 Circumnavigation Discovery">
    <h2>4. History &amp; Magellan's 1522 Circumnavigation Discovery</h2>
    <p>
      The physical necessity of an International Date Line was first discovered during Ferdinand Magellan's historic circumnavigation of the globe (1519–1522).
    </p>
    <p>
      Magellan's ship, the <em>Victoria</em>, sailed continuously westward from Spain around South America and across the Pacific Ocean. The crew kept meticulous daily logbooks counting every single sunrise and sunset.
    </p>
    <p>
      However, when the surviving crew reached Cape Verde off the coast of Africa in July 1522, their ship log recorded the date as <strong>Wednesday, July 9</strong>. To their complete bewilderment, land officials insisted the date was <strong>Thursday, July 10</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Because the ship had traveled westward around the entire planet, the crew had experienced one fewer solar sunrise relative to stationary land observers! This historical revelation proved that a global date line was mathematically essential.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 5 -->
  <section id="zigzag-deviations" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Why the Date Line Zigzags">
    <h2>5. Why the Date Line Zigzags (Kiribati &amp; Samoa Case Studies)</h2>
    <p>
      If the International Date Line were a straight 180° meridian line, it would split several island nations into two different calendar days simultaneously! 
    </p>

    <h3>Case Study 1: Kiribati (1994)</h3>
    <p>
      The island nation of Kiribati spans 3,500 kilometers across the central Pacific. Prior to 1994, the IDL cut directly through the middle of the country. As a result, the western Gilbert Islands were on Tuesday while the eastern Line Islands were on Monday, making national business communication impossible for 4 days out of every week!
    </p>
    <p>
      On December 31, 1994, Kiribati moved the Date Line 3,000 km eastward around its territory, creating the world's earliest time zone: <strong>UTC+14:00 (Line Islands Time)</strong>. Kiribati became the first nation to welcome the new millennium on January 1, 2000.
    </p>

    <h3>Case Study 2: Samoa (2011)</h3>
    <p>
      Historically, Samoa traded primarily with California and operated on UTC-11 (east of the Date Line). However, by 2011, Samoa's economy became heavily dependent on trade with Australia and New Zealand.
    </p>
    <p>
      Because Samoa was on yesterday's date, when it was Friday in Sydney, it was Thursday in Samoa. When it was Monday in Samoa, it was Tuesday in Sydney—leaving only 3 shared business workdays per week.
    </p>
    <p>
      On December 29, 2011, Samoa jumped across the Date Line by erasing Friday, December 30, 2011 from its calendar! Citizens went to sleep on Thursday night and woke up on Saturday morning, shifting permanently to <strong>UTC+13:00</strong>. See details in <a href="/australia-time-zones/">Australia Time Zones Guide</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 6 -->
  <section id="diomede-islands" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Tomorrow Island vs Yesterday Island">
    <h2>6. Tomorrow Island vs Yesterday Island (Bering Strait)</h2>
    <p>
      The most famous geographical anomaly of the International Date Line occurs in the Bering Strait between Alaska and Siberia:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Big Diomede Island (Russia):</strong> Known as "Tomorrow Island," operating on UTC+12:00.</li>
      <li><strong>Little Diomede Island (US):</strong> Known as "Yesterday Island," operating on UTC-9:00 (AKST).</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      These two islands are separated by just <strong>2.4 miles (3.8 km)</strong> of water. Yet because the International Date Line passes directly between them, a person standing on Little Diomede looking across the water at Big Diomede is looking <strong>21 hours into the future!</strong>
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 7 -->
  <section id="aviation-travel-math" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Aviation Flight Math and Double New Year">
    <h2>7. Aviation Flight Math &amp; Double New Year's Celebrations</h2>
    <p>
      Transpacific air travel produces fascinating chronological phenomena:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Landing Before You Depart:</strong> Air New Zealand Flight NZ2 operates from Auckland (UTC+13) to <a href="/los-angeles-time/">Los Angeles</a> (UTC-7). If a flight departs Auckland at 8:00 PM on Tuesday, after a 12-hour flight across the Date Line, it lands in Los Angeles at 1:00 PM on Tuesday—7 hours <em>before</em> it departed local clock time!
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Ringing in New Year's Twice:</strong> Partygoers routinely charter flights from Auckland or Samoa to Honolulu on January 1, allowing them to celebrate midnight New Year's Eve twice in a single 24-hour period.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Master conversion steps in <a href="/how-to-convert-time-zones/">How to Convert Time Zones</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 8 -->
  <section id="utc-range-matrix" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Extreme Offset Range UTC-12 to UTC+14">
    <h2>8. Extreme Offset Range: UTC-12:00 vs UTC+14:00</h2>
    <p>
      The geographical zigzagging of the Date Line expands the total offset range across Earth to 26 hours (from UTC-12:00 to UTC+14:00).
    </p>
    <p>
      This means that for 2 hours every day (between 10:00 UTC and 12:00 UTC), three different calendar dates exist simultaneously across the globe:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>In Kiribati (UTC+14), the time is 00:00 on <strong>Thursday</strong>.</li>
      <li>In London (UTC+0), the time is 10:00 AM on <strong>Wednesday</strong>.</li>
      <li>In Baker Island (UTC-12), the time is 22:00 (10:00 PM) on <strong>Tuesday</strong>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Learn about ISO 8601 timestamps in <a href="/what-is-utc-offset/">What is a UTC Offset?</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 9: DATE LINE MATRIX -->
  <section id="date-line-matrix" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Major Pacific Region Date Line Comparison Table">
    <h2>9. Major Pacific Region Date Line Comparison Table</h2>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Region / Country</th>
            <th>Side of Date Line</th>
            <th>UTC Offset</th>
            <th>Calendar Date relative to US</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Auckland (New Zealand)</strong></td>
            <td>West (Asia/Pacific)</td>
            <td>UTC+12 / UTC+13</td>
            <td>+1 Calendar Day ahead of US</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Kiritimati (Kiribati)</strong></td>
            <td>West (Furthest East Bend)</td>
            <td>UTC+14:00</td>
            <td>+1 Calendar Day ahead of US (First to see sunrise)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Samoa</strong></td>
            <td>West (Jumped in 2011)</td>
            <td>UTC+13:00</td>
            <td>+1 Calendar Day ahead of US</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>American Samoa (US)</strong></td>
            <td>East (Americas)</td>
            <td>UTC-11:00</td>
            <td>Same calendar date as US West Coast</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Honolulu (Hawaii)</strong></td>
            <td>East (Americas)</td>
            <td>UTC-10:00</td>
            <td>Same calendar date as US West Coast</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 10: FAQS -->
  <section id="faq-section" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Frequently Asked Questions">
    <h2>10. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)</h2>
